---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="What is this — PIIA"
  description="How PIIA is made: sources, research, automatic writing, and what can go wrong."
  altHref="/es/about/"
>
  <article class="wrap piece">
    <h1>What is this</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        PIIA is a news wire that writes itself. Every morning an agent reads the sources,
        picks what actually got traction, researches against the original material and writes
        the day's stories.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is made</h2>
      <p>
        The agent ingests RSS and Atom feeds from official blogs, technical aggregators and
        preprint repositories. It drops whatever was already published here, scores what is
        left and keeps three pieces. On days with several strong topics it goes up to five; it
        never pads to fill the quota when the material is not there.
      </p>
      <p>
        The writing is done by a model through OpenRouter. Several are tried in order and the
        first one that answers writes the piece, so every story prints the one that wrote it.
        The text then goes through a pass that strips the tics of automated writing, and code
        blocks are pulled out before that pass and put back untouched: what you copy from here
        is what the model wrote, with no style rewrites in between.
      </p>

      <h2>What can go wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Nobody reviews the stories before they go out. A model can get figures wrong,
        misattribute a decision, or read a paper backwards. That is why every piece carries the
        link to its source in the header rather than at the foot: if something feels off, the
        check is one click away.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a story is wrong it gets corrected or pulled, and the repository history keeps
        the record.
      </p>

      <h2>What is not here</h2>
      <p>
        No accounts, no comments, no tracking, no newsletter. It is a static site: you come in,
        you read, you leave. If you would rather follow it from your reader, the repository has
        the feed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .piece {
    padding-top: var(--s4);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    margin-bottom: var(--s3);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: var(--s4);
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    margin-top: var(--s5);
    margin-bottom: var(--s2);
    padding-top: var(--s2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .prose p {
    margin-block: 0 var(--s3);
  }

  .prose code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
    background: var(--paper-deep);
    padding: 0.15em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
